1:/// The `record_tag` wire protocol (GOAL #2394, Compaction 2.0 v2.1).
2:///
3:/// Every visible session record rides the wire prefixed with its file
4:/// alias — `m<seq> ` (`m142 toolResult(bash): …`) — so the model can
5:/// ADDRESS records in a hand-over reply ("hide 142-143") and the engine
6:/// can resolve those numbers back to stable record ids through the
7:/// SNAPSHOT index of the request the model actually saw.
8:///
9:/// Invariants (I4):
10:/// - a tag is wire-only: it is stamped on a COPY of the transcript right
11:///   before the request transforms, never on the transcript itself, never
12:///   persisted (`Context.toJson` is untouched);
13:/// - inside a tag epoch (the projection the transcript was rebuilt from)
14:///   the head messages carry their exact alias and the bytes are stable;
15:///   messages appended after the epoch started (the run's live edge —
16:///   the prompt, nudges, this run's answers and tool results) carry NO
17:///   tag: they are not records of this epoch yet (persistence is
18:///   asynchronous) and the model does not address them. The next epoch
19:///   (the next run's projection rebuild) tags them — one uncached
20:///   segment at the run boundary (E30), visible in the main request's
21:///   cache metric and the accepted conservative default of the card;
22:/// - non-records (the obligations / working-set tail blocks, `time-left:`
23:///   lines, carrier pins, the hand-over instruction) never carry a tag;
24:/// - tags exist only under the structured engine (`compaction.engine`).
25:///
26:/// Pure Dart — no IO; the transcript seq map is built by the session
27:/// projection (`Session.buildTranscriptProjection`).
