1:/// User-facing compaction trigger tuning (gh-2098): the `compaction:`
2:/// yaml keys override the gh-1742 `CompactionSettings.forWindow` curves.
3:///
4:/// gh-1742 shipped the hard-coded threshold policy — provider-usage
5:/// anchored estimates plus proportional headroom
6:/// (`reserve = min(65536, max(window ~/ 16, legacy curve))`). Three gaps
7:/// remained: the trigger is not user-configurable, the ~94% default
8:/// fires late on large-window model classes, and the only lever was
9:/// lying about the window (`agent.contextWindowCap` also moves the
10:/// over-window guard). This library adds the config seam:
11:///
12:/// - `compaction.triggerRatio` (0 < r < 1, e.g. 0.75): the trigger line
13:///   sits at `r × window` — the reserve becomes `window − floor(r ×
14:///   window)`, so EVERY window-derived consumer (both trigger channels,
15:///   the structured engine's gate, the ctx meter, the spill budget)
16:///   moves together through the one reserve value.
17:/// - `compaction.reserveTokens` / `compaction.keepRecentTokens`: direct
18:///   overrides of the forWindow curve outputs. `triggerRatio` and
19:///   `reserveTokens` are two spellings of the same knob (the trigger
20:///   line) — configuring both is a strict config error.
21:/// - Absent keys → the gh-1742 behavior byte-identically
22:///   ([applyCompactionTuning] returns the base instance untouched).
23:///
24:/// The default decision (documented in `docs/compaction.md` with the
25:/// benchmark note): the legacy gh-1742 curve stays the default — moving
26:/// every session to the 0.75–0.80 band multiplies compaction frequency
27:/// and cost; operators of large-window classes opt in per scope.
28:///
29:/// Pure Dart — no dart:io; the yaml surface parses in the CLI config
30:/// layer (`cli_config.dart`), the application is shared host wiring
31:/// (`host_wiring.dart`).
